  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis place the UP on the \nnortheastern periphery of a strong mid level ridge over the central \nCONUS. At the sfc weak high pressure around 1016 mb is centered \nover northern IL. This is extending weak 1012-1015 mb ridging north-\nnortheast over the UP, Ontario, and into northern Quebec. As a \nresult of the southwest winds and height rises, temps warm up today \ninto the 80s to low 90s. With this WAA and moisture advection, humid \nconditions and modest instability develops supportive of storms. \nTiming is a bit blurry as convection has to move over a more stable \nlake influenced airmass and forcing is somewhat nebulous, but CAMs \nindicate the moisture convergence and weak dynamics will support \nsome showers and weak storm activity over the west. Likely start \ntime window is 12-15Z timeframe, but a few isolated showers may \nsneak in ahead of that. These showers/storms continue to expand and \npress eastward before dissipating toward the later afternoon hours \nas a disturbance over ND and Manitoba approaches. With lackluster \nforcing and unimpressive shear, severe weather is not expected. \nHowever, heavy downpours yielding isolated rainfall amounts up to \n0.5\" to 0.75\" is possible (30-60% chance); mean rainfall totals of \n0.25\" or lower are anticipated, highest west half. Otherwise patchy \nwildfire smoke from the northwestern CONUS may yield hazy skies \ntoday into the weekend.\n\nA mid level trough over the Dakotas at the end of today moves over \nWI Friday into Saturday, interacting with a descending cold front \nfrom the northwest. This yields better widespread chances (40-70%) \nfor showers and storms on Friday. Shear will continue to be lacking, \nkeeping the strong to severe threat at bay. This round supports \nbetter QPF fields with NBM depicting more widespread coverage of \namounts in the 0.25\" to 0.5\". Temps fall back into the 70s to low \n80s on Friday.\n\nA few showers and thunderstorms could linger into early Saturday in \nthe wake of these systems, mainly east half. Do not expect any \nstrong to severe storms for any of these periods as shear remains \nweak for the next few days. Sunday into Monday favors drier weather \na high pressure builds back into the region, with temperatures \nlooking to climb back into the 80s for much of next week. Another \napproaching trough will bring increasing precipitation chances again \ntowards the middle of next week, but details are sparse at this \ntime.",
